================
DHCP Starvation Attack

Why we did this
================
Before learning how to defend against DHCP attacks, administrators must first understand how attackers exploit DHCP.

DHCP servers have a limited pool of IP addresses.

An attacker can repeatedly send thousands of DHCP Discover requests using fake MAC addresses.

The DHCP Server believes each request comes from a new device and starts assigning IP addresses.

Eventually, the entire DHCP pool becomes exhausted.

When a legitimate user connects, no IP addresses remain available.

This attack is called DHCP Starvation.

What will happen
================
1. Attacker sends thousands of DHCP Discover packets.
2. Each request uses a different fake MAC address.
3. DHCP Server allocates IP addresses for each request.
4. DHCP pool runs out of available addresses.
5. Legitimate users cannot obtain an IP address.
6. Users lose network connectivity.
7. Attackers may then introduce a Rogue DHCP Server.

Example

DHCP Pool:
-----------
192.168.1.1 - 192.168.1.254

Attacker:
---------
Generates 254 fake DHCP requests

Result:
--------
All available addresses consumed

Legitimate User:
----------------
No IP Address Available

Network Impact:
---------------
Connectivity Failure

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages (For Learning Purposes)
----------------------------------
1. Helps understand DHCP weaknesses.
2. Demonstrates how DHCP services can be attacked.
3. Useful for security awareness and training.
4. Shows the importance of DHCP Snooping.
5. Helps validate security controls.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Exhausts DHCP address pools.
2. Causes Denial of Service (DoS).
3. Legitimate users cannot obtain IP addresses.
4. May lead to major business disruptions.
5. Can be used as a precursor to Rogue DHCP attacks.
6. Impacts network availability.

Hence what came next
--------------------
After identifying DHCP Starvation attacks, administrators needed a defense mechanism.

Cisco introduced:

DHCP Snooping Rate Limiting

which limits the number of DHCP requests allowed from a port.

If excessive DHCP requests are detected:
- Packets are dropped
- Violations are triggered
- DHCP pool remains protected

This mitigation is commonly referred to as:

DHCP Starvation Mitigation

One-Shot Lab Configuration
==========================

Vulnerable Network Setup

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access

DHCP Server
-----------
Configured normally with a limited DHCP pool.

Attacker Machine
----------------
Connected to Fa0/1 and generates thousands of DHCP requests using random MAC addresses.

Verification
============

show ip dhcp binding

show ip dhcp snooping binding

show ip dhcp snooping statistics


Expected Result
===============

Before Attack:
--------------
Free IP addresses available.

During Attack:
--------------
DHCP pool rapidly consumed.

After Attack:
-------------
No IP addresses available.

Legitimate Users:
-----------------
Unable to obtain DHCP lease.

Network State:
--------------
Denial of Service condition.

Summary
=======

Problem:
DHCP servers have a limited number of available IP addresses.

Attack:
An attacker sends thousands of DHCP requests using fake MAC addresses until all addresses are consumed.

Impact:
Legitimate users cannot obtain IP addresses and lose connectivity.

Limitation:
The network remains vulnerable until protection mechanisms are implemented.

